    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d15">

                <pb facs="0080" n="894" sameAs="frus:frus1952-54v02p2/pg894"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">330.13/4–1552</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by the Assistant Secretary of State for
                        United Nations Affairs (Hickerson) to the Secretary of
                            State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">confidential</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1952-04-17">April 17, 1952</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <list subtype="subject" type="description">
                    <item>Subject:</item>
                    <item>Disarmament: Memorandum of April 15, 1952 from Mr. Cohen<anchor n="1" xml:id="d15-f1"
                        /></item>
                </list>
                <p>I have the following comments on the five points mentioned in the attached
                    memorandum from Mr. Cohen to you: <list>
                        <item>Point 1. I agree with Mr. Cohen that it would be helpful if the President should
                            indicate to the Secretary of Defense and probably also to General Bradley his continued
                            interest in the development of a constructive and comprehensive program
                            of disarmament. I believe that such an indication would be more
                            effective if it were on an informal and confidential basis. For example,
                            it might be desirable for the President to bring the matter up
                            informally at a meeting of the National Security Council. It should be
                            stressed that operating relationships between the Department of State
                            and the Department of Defense on this subject are on a cordial and
                            cooperative basis. We need to ensure that the officers of the Department
                            of Defense adopt a more positive attitude despite the fact that they are
                            participating in an activity which, in general, is unpopular in
                            Defense.</item>
                        <item>Points 2, 3 and 4. Mr. Cohen has been informed of the steps that have been
                            taken and that we plan to take on these matters, and which generally
                            parallel his recommendations.</item>
                        <item>Point 5. We have explored these ideas both in the Department and with
                            other Agencies but nothing has yet materialized.</item>
                    </list></p>



                <xi:include href="d15-a1.xml" xpointer="d15-a1"/>
            </div>
